NAME
       hostnamectl - show or change the system's host name

SYNOPSIS
       hostnamectl [status]
       hostnamectl hostname [NAME]
       hostnamectl set-hostname NAME [--static|--transient]

DESCRIPTION
       hostnamectl talks to systemd-hostnamed, which looks after the host
       name and a few details about the machine. It is the RHEL 9 way to set
       the name permanently.

       systemd knows three names:

       static     the one stored in /etc/hostname, used at every boot;

       transient  the name the kernel uses right now (normally the same as
                  the static one; DHCP or the hostname command can change it
                  until the next boot);

       pretty     a free-form description for people ("Andrew's laptop").

       Setting a name with hostnamectl writes /etc/hostname and changes the
       running name at once. Shells that are already open keep showing the
       old name in their prompt until you start a new one (exec bash, or log
       in again). hostnamectl does not touch /etc/hosts or DNS: other machines
       learn the name only if you add it there.

COMMANDS
       status
              Show the names and some facts about the machine: machine ID,
              boot ID, virtualization, operating system, kernel, architecture.
              This is the default when no command is given.

       hostname [NAME]
              With no NAME, print the current host name. With a NAME, set it
              (the same as set-hostname).

       set-hostname NAME
              Set the static and transient names to NAME. Only root (or a
              user who can authenticate as an administrator) can do this.

OPTIONS
       --static, --transient, --pretty
              With status, print only that name. With set-hostname, change
              only that name: --static writes /etc/hostname without renaming
              the running system; --transient renames the running system
              until the next boot.

EXAMPLES
       Give server1 its full name and check it:
              # hostnamectl set-hostname server1.lab.example.com
              # hostnamectl hostname
              server1.lab.example.com
              # cat /etc/hostname
              server1.lab.example.com

       Show the full status:
              $ hostnamectl
               Static hostname: server1.lab.example.com
                     Icon name: computer-vm
                       Chassis: vm
              ...
              Operating System: Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9.5 (Plow)

       Start a new shell so the prompt shows the new name:
              # exec bash

FILES
       /etc/hostname
              The static host name, one line.

       /etc/machine-info
              The pretty name and other optional details.

SEE ALSO
       hostname(1), hostname(5), hosts(5), nmcli(1), systemd-hostnamed.service(8)
